112
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
that I think will be very useful to you

113
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
as a scout master or a scouting volunteer.

114
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
So, hey, without further ado, let's get started, shall we?

115
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
And now our next episode of the Colorado experiment.

116
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:39,000
This is our second installment of the Colorado experiment.

117
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
I love the idea that it's an experiment

118
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
That's awesome,

119
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
And that's Arlen Ward,

120
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
And he's out there in Colorado

121
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
And the experiment is, I think, at one point,

122
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Arlen. we call it jumping into the volcano.

123
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Yeah,

124
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
I think that's an apt description, as

125
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:58,000
I think it was Walter that said:

126
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
you're just not ever the same after you jump into the volcano.

127
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Right,

128
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
In case you haven't been following the story,

129
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
jumping into the volcano is a euphemism we're using

130
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
for becoming a scout master.

131
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
And not only is Arlen a brand new scout master,

132
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
but he's also got a brand new troop

133
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
And we talked a little bit the last time we were together

134
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
last month about how that came together

135
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
And we've been talking a little bit here

136
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:27,000
before we started recording.

137
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,000
but, Arlen, you've had your first patrol leader's council meeting.

138
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Yeah,

139
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
So actually we've had two PLC meetings

140
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
because we've kind of worked our way through the first month,

141
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
which has been interesting.

142
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
So who is at this patrol leader's council meeting

143
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:44,000
that you're having?

144
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
Well, the very first one we had,

145
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
we had talked to a number of scouts of different ages

146
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,000
and we had two older, 16 year old life scouts

147
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,000
that said that they really liked the idea of starting a troop

148
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
and setting the tone and the culture of the troop

149
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,000
from the beginning,

150
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
And so we had the two of them was really the first meeting

151
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:07,000
that we had.

152
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Obviously, from your experience level and your age

153
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,000
and your interest in really setting this,

154
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
I would like you to be involved

155
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
in leadership positions in the troop

156
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
And so why don't you join us one night

157
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
and we'll sit here and we'll talk about it?

158
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
So it was myself, the committee chair,

159
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
one of the assistant scout masters.

160
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
We kind of sat together on one end

161
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
and then there was these two life scouts

162
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,000
that sat together on the other side

163
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
And these two guys are great.

164
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
They have known each other since Tiger Cubs

165
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
are great friends.

166
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
They know a lot about scouting

167
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
and what's been going on in their own experience.

168
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,000
The troop is still kind of in this formative stages.

169
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Is one of these guys your senior patrol leader now,

170
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
or have you formalized anything like that?

171
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
That was one of the first things that came up

172
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:09,000
that I was like: okay, this is definitely not my troop,

173
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
It's up to them, because that's what I had said to them.

174
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
I said: the two of you,

175
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
or what do you want to do with regards to troop level leadership?

176
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
And they talked about it for a little bit

177
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
and they came back to me and said:

178
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
we want to be co-senior patrol leaders.

179
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,000
And the committee chair looked at me: can they do that?

180
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
I was like I've never heard of it,

181
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,000
I've never seen it, but I don't see why not.

182
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
I said you have to be prepared for the time

183
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
that the two of you don't agree on something.

184
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
And they seemed rather unconcerned with that.

185
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
But I said: yeah, I have no problem with that.

186
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
If you want to both be senior patrol leader,

187
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
knock yourself out.

188
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
There will be elections and whatnot

189
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
at some point in the future.

190
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
This is the way that they wanted to move forward.

191
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
You're comfortable with that?

192
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Yes,

193
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,000
Okay,

194
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
I didn't see a reason to stand in their way.

195
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
I should point out to everybody

196
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
that should be everybody's watchword right there.

197
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
I didn't see a reason to stand in their way.

198
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
That would be a really good place to start.

199
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So it's not worrying you that this isn't following

200
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
some pattern that maybe you had in your mind

201
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
or the other adults had in their mind?

202
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Are you guys overly concerned about having elections

203
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
or formalizing things a little bit more,

204
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
so it looks like the book,

205
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,000
Or are you comfortable with moving forward this way?

206
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
When we talked about it initially,

207
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,000
I said: if it doesn't work, we can always change it,

208
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:36,000
And as it sits now

209
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:41,000
we are looking at the first set of elections a year out-

210
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
The size of a troop. there's plenty of leadership opportunities.

211
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:48,000
I'm not worried about anybody not having their six months

212
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
for whatever advancement that they need.

213
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
I want to capture one other phrase.

214
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:53,000
The first phrase is:

215
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
I didn't really see any reason to stand in their way,

216
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:57,000
And the second one is:

217
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
if it doesn't work out, we can change it.

218
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Yeah,

219
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,000
And neither one of those are unique phrases

220
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,000
that I came up with

221
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
And I learned all this stuff from you,

222
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
from other scoutmasters.

223
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
There's a scoutmaster in our area that I had mentioned last time.

224
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
I invited us down to talk about his program

225
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
and what they do in their troop,

226
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
And one of the things in Wood Badger they talk about

227
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
is the stages of team development.

228
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
If you significantly change the sort of thing that you're doing,

229
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
you go through those same stages all over again.

230
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
So we have some idea of the dynamic

231
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
of the relationship with the adults

232
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
and these youth leaders.

233
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Right now you're working with two pretty motivated youth leaders

234
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and who are excited at the prospect of what's happening.

235
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
What are the adults doing

236
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
at the Petroleum Council meeting?

237
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
Honestly, I think that was probably a mistake

238
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
to have them there.

239
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Now, why do you say that

240
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:00,000
They stepped in? probably a little bit more than I would have,

241
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Just providing answers rather than asking questions.

242
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
You don't have a transcript of the meeting, obviously,

243
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
As that dialogue was going on.

244
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,000
give me an idea of what you consider to be like

245
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,000
outside of the bounds of what you would like to have happen.

246
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Well, I can kind of give you the whole dynamic of that

247
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
in one topic,

248
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,000
And this shows that these two scouts

249
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
aren't any different than anybody else's. I guess

250
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
They had brought up uniforms.

251
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
Well, what are we going to do about uniforms?

252
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:38,000
And we started talking about it in general terms

253
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
and one of them said: you know,

254
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
I was kind of hoping that we wouldn't need uniforms.

255
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
He's 16 years old.

256
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
I remember being a scout at 16..

257
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
